WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.579
Which model for which stage.

2
00:00:01.859 --> 00:00:07.469
Different stages need different amounts of capability, and the price spread between tiers is large.

3
00:00:07.749 --> 00:00:12.741
On the Claude pricing page as of today, per million tokens, Claude Opus 5.

4
00:00:13.021 --> 00:00:18.013
5 costs $4 for input and $20 for output, Claude Sonnet 5.

5
00:00:18.293 --> 00:00:21.856
5 costs $2 and $10, and Claude Haiku 5.

6
00:00:22.136 --> 00:00:23.693
5 costs $0.

7
00:00:23.973 --> 00:00:25.403
10 and $0.

8
00:00:25.683 --> 00:00:29.800
50 (for prompts up to 100,000 tokens).

9
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.451
The Batch API halves those prices for work that can wait.

10
00:00:33.731 --> 00:00:37.315
Prices change, so check the page before you rely on these.

11
00:00:37.595 --> 00:00:41.285
Now a back-of-envelope estimate, with every assumption stated.

12
00:00:41.565 --> 00:00:46.088
Assume a one-hour meeting transcript of about 9,000 words.

13
00:00:46.368 --> 00:01:03.221
Using OpenAI’s rough rule of about three quarters of a word per token, that is about 12,000 tokens; add about 2,000 for instructions and the output schema, so 14,000 input tokens per meeting.

14
00:01:03.501 --> 00:01:11.096
Assume the extraction returns 20 candidates at about 75 tokens each, so 1,500 output tokens.

15
00:01:11.376 --> 00:01:13.659
Then one extraction call costs about 8.

16
00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:15.496
6 cents on Opus 5.

17
00:01:15.776 --> 00:01:16.843
5, 4.

18
00:01:17.123 --> 00:01:18.765
3 cents on Sonnet 5.

19
00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:20.496
5, and about 0.

20
00:01:20.776 --> 00:01:22.376
2 cents on Haiku 5.

21
00:01:22.656 --> 00:01:23.424
5.

22
00:01:23.704 --> 00:01:28.269
At 200 meetings a month, that is roughly $17, $8.

23
00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:30.171
60, and $0.

24
00:01:30.451 --> 00:01:31.368
43.

25
00:01:31.648 --> 00:01:33.397
Two cautions keep this honest.

26
00:01:33.677 --> 00:01:38.797
First, on current Claude models thinking tokens are billed as output, and Opus 5.

27
00:01:39.077 --> 00:01:44.539
5 always thinks, so real output usage can be well above 1,500 tokens.

28
00:01:44.819 --> 00:01:46.696
The effort setting changes how much.

29
00:01:46.976 --> 00:01:52.608
Measure usage from real responses (every response reports it) and replace these guesses.

30
00:01:52.888 --> 00:01:54.744
Second, look at what the numbers say.

31
00:01:55.024 --> 00:02:04.048
At this volume, the most expensive option likely costs less per month than an hour of a reviewer’s time, and a missed commitment can cost far more than that.

32
00:02:04.328 --> 00:02:07.421
Cost is a constraint here, not the deciding factor.

33
00:02:07.701 --> 00:02:09.237
So decide with evidence.

34
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:14.253
Label a set of transcripts with the action items a careful person would extract.

35
00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:20.933
Run the extraction stage on two or three candidate models, and compare recall (did it find the real items?)

36
00:02:21.213 --> 00:02:23.645
, precision (are the items it found real?)

37
00:02:23.925 --> 00:02:25.931
, and the quote-verification pass rate.

38
00:02:26.211 --> 00:02:28.515
Pick the cheapest model that meets your bar.

39
00:02:28.795 --> 00:02:37.520
Then do the same per stage: deduplicating ambiguous pairs is a much smaller judgment than extraction, and a small model often suffices there.

40
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:50.173
The same method applies if you compare Claude with GPT, Gemini, Grok, or an open-weight model you host yourself, which some teams choose when transcripts must not leave their own infrastructure.

41
00:02:50.453 --> 00:02:57.280
Behavior and cost differ by family and version; your eval is what tells you which differences matter for this task.
